Notebook[{

Cell["Additive Channels in Curved Fitness Landscapes — Expanded Annotated Mathematica Notebook", "Title"],

Cell["Overview", "Section"],
Cell["This notebook is a saveable working document for checking, extending, and illustrating the additive-channels framework in Mathematica. It is written as a readable derivation notebook rather than a minimal script. The aim is to keep the algebra transparent, the assumptions explicit, and the transition from symbolic formulas to numerical examples easy to follow.", "Text"],

Cell["What this notebook contains", "Section"],
Cell["The notebook is organised in eight blocks.\n\n1. Setup and conventions.\n2. Local variance decomposition under Gaussian closure.\n3. The additivity index Ag as a local R^2.\n4. The logit transform and the rate diagnostic r(t).\n5. The one-trait moving-optimum model.\n6. The two-trait gradient-rotation model.\n7. A symbolic covariance-dynamics closure and the mechanistic decomposition of r.\n8. The introgression criterion and a few export-quality plots.\n\nEach section contains background text, code, and notes on what to record for the manuscript.", "Text"],

Cell["How to run this notebook", "Section"],
Cell["Open this file in Mathematica or Wolfram Desktop and evaluate from top to bottom with Evaluation ▶ Evaluate Notebook, or run one section at a time with Shift+Enter. The symbolic cells are written so that they can usually be re-evaluated independently once the setup section has been run. The plotting cells are grouped near the end so that you can regenerate figures after changing parameter blocks.", "Text"],

Cell["Why Mathematica is a good fit", "Section"],
Cell["For this project Mathematica is a good primary environment because it handles symbolic differentiation, matrix calculus, assumptions, and notebook-style exposition in one place. A Python/SymPy notebook is a reasonable secondary option, especially for version control and larger simulation pipelines, but for line-by-line symbolic checking and presentation, Mathematica is the stronger primary tool.", "Text"],

Cell["1. Setup and conventions", "Section"],
Cell["Clear the global namespace before starting.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ClearAll","[","\"Global`*\"","]"}]], "Input"],

Cell["A small display helper.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"divider","[","s_","]"}],":=",
  RowBox[{"Style","[",
   RowBox[{"s",",","15",",","Bold",",",
    RowBox[{"Darker","[","Blue","]"}]}],"]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["We work with the local second-order expansion of log-fitness around the current population mean. In the notation of the paper, deltaL = L + Q, where L = b^T Delta z is the local linear term and Q = (1/2) Delta z^T H Delta z is the quadratic correction. Throughout, G is the additive genetic covariance matrix, b is the local gradient of log-fitness, and H is the local Hessian.", "Text"],

Cell["2. Variance decomposition under Gaussian closure", "Section"],
Cell["Start with a concrete 2-trait example. This lets us check the formulas numerically before moving to general symbolic expressions.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"G2","=","{{1.0,0.3},{0.3,0.6}}"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"b2","=","{0.8,0.4}"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"H2","=","{{-0.4,0},{0,-0.4}}"}],";"}]], "Input"],

Cell["Compute the variance components directly from the definitions in the manuscript.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Vlin2","=",RowBox[{"Simplify","[",RowBox[{"b2",".","G2",".","b2"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Vquad2","=",RowBox[{"Simplify","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",
       RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"H2",".","G2"}],".",RowBox[{"H2",".","G2"}]}],"]"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Ag2","=",RowBox[{"N","[",RowBox[{"Vlin2","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"Vlin2","+","Vquad2"}],")"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"Vlin\"","->","Vlin2"}],",",RowBox[{"\"Vquad\"","->","Vquad2"}],",",RowBox[{"\"Ag\"","->","Ag2"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["This should return Vlin = 0.928, Vquad = 0.1232, and Ag about 0.883.", "Text"],

Cell["Now verify the same result by Monte Carlo simulation from a centred multivariate normal with covariance G.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"SeedRandom","[","12345","]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"nSamp","=","200000"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"samples","=",RowBox[{"RandomVariate","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"MultinormalDistribution","[",RowBox[{"{0,0}",",","G2"}],"]"}],",","nSamp"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Lvals","=",RowBox[{"samples",".","b2"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Qvals","=",RowBox[{"Map","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"#",".","H2",".","#"}]}],"&",",","samples"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"deltaVals","=",RowBox[{"Lvals","+","Qvals"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\"Var[L] empirical\"","->",RowBox[{"N","@",RowBox[{"Variance","[","Lvals","]"}]}]}],",",
    RowBox[{"\"Var[Q] empirical\"","->",RowBox[{"N","@",RowBox[{"Variance","[","Qvals","]"}]}]}],",",
    RowBox[{"\"Cov[L,Q] empirical\"","->",RowBox[{"N","@",RowBox[{"Covariance","[",RowBox[{"Lvals",",","Qvals"}],"]"}]}]}],",",
    RowBox[{"\"Var[L+Q] empirical\"","->",RowBox[{"N","@",RowBox[{"Variance","[","deltaVals","]"}]}]}],",",
    RowBox[{"\"Var[L]+Var[Q]\"","->",RowBox[{"N","@",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Variance","[","Lvals","]"}],"+",RowBox[{"Variance","[","Qvals","]"}]}],")"}]}]}]}],
   "}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["Interpretation: under Gaussian closure, Cov[L,Q] should be close to zero, and the variance of L+Q should match the sum of the two component variances.", "Text"],

Cell["General symbolic formulas", "Subsection"],
Cell["Write down the general expressions used later in the notebook. In this block, the formulas are entered directly, rather than derived from Expectation, because that is the clean form needed for the manuscript.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"n","=","3"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"b","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"bb",",","n"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"G","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"g",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"n",",","n"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"H","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"h",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"n",",","n"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VlinGeneral","=",RowBox[{"b",".","G",".","b"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VquadGeneral","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"H",".","G"}],".",RowBox[{"H",".","G"}]}],"]"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"AgGeneral","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",RowBox[{"VlinGeneral","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"VlinGeneral","+","VquadGeneral"}],")"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"Vlin general\"","->","VlinGeneral"}],",",RowBox[{"\"Vquad general\"","->","VquadGeneral"}],",",RowBox[{"\"Ag general\"","->","AgGeneral"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["3. The additivity index as a local R^2", "Section"],
Cell["Under Gaussian closure, Ag should equal Corr[L, L+Q]^2. Check that numerically in the same 2-trait system.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"corrSq","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Correlation","[",RowBox[{"Lvals",",","deltaVals"}],"]"}],"^","2"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"Ag from variance ratio\"","->","N[Ag2]"}],",",RowBox[{"\"Corr[L,L+Q]^2\"","->","N[corrSq]"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["4. The logit transform and the rate diagnostic", "Section"],
Cell["Define Llogit = log(Vlin/Vquad). Then r(t) = dLlogit/dt. The next cell proves the exact identity dAg/dt = Ag (1-Ag) r.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ClearAll","[",RowBox[{"VlinT",",","VquadT",",","t"}],"]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"AgT","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinT","[","t","]"}],"/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinT","[","t","]"}],"+",RowBox[{"VquadT","[","t","]"}]}],")"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"LlogitT","=",RowBox[{"Log","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinT","[","t","]"}],"/",RowBox[{"VquadT","[","t","]"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"rT","=",RowBox[{"D","[",RowBox[{"LlogitT",",","t"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"identityCheck","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"D","[",RowBox[{"AgT",",","t"}],"]"}],"-",RowBox[{"AgT"," ",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"1","-","AgT"}],")"}]," ","rT"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"r(t)\"","->","rT"}],",",RowBox[{"\"dAg/dt - Ag(1-Ag)r\"","->","identityCheck"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["The second output should simplify to 0.", "Text"],

Cell["5. One-trait moving-optimum model", "Section"],
Cell["Encode the scalar model l(z,t) = l0 - (1/2) s (z - theta)^2. The important objects are the lag d = theta - zbar, the variance G, the additivity index Ag, and the rate diagnostic r.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ClearAll","[",RowBox[{"s",",","d",",","Gsc",",","thetaDot",",","lambdaRec",",","Gstar",",","m",",","Ne"}],"]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Vlin1D","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"s"," ","d"}],")"}],"^","2"," ","Gsc"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Vquad1D","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"s","^","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"Gsc","^","2"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Ag1D","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",RowBox[{"Vlin1D","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"Vlin1D","+","Vquad1D"}],")"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"r1D","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"2"," ",RowBox[{"thetaDot","/","d"}]}],"-",RowBox[{"s"," ","Gsc"}],"+",RowBox[{"lambdaRec"," ",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"1","-",RowBox[{"Gstar","/","Gsc"}]}],")"}]}],"-",RowBox[{"m","/","Gsc"}],"+",RowBox[{"1","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"2"," ","Ne"}],")"}]}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"Ag(d,G)\"","->","Ag1D"}],",",RowBox[{"\"r\"","->","r1D"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["This should return Ag = d^2/(d^2 + G/2), showing that the stabilising curvature strength s cancels in the ratio.", "Text"],

Cell["Baseline numerical values", "Subsection"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"params1D","=",RowBox[{"<|",
    RowBox[{RowBox[{"s","->","0.10"}],",",RowBox[{"d","->","2.0"}],",",RowBox[{"Gsc","->","1.0"}],",",RowBox[{"thetaDot","->","0.02"}],",",RowBox[{"lambdaRec","->","0.30"}],",",RowBox[{"Gstar","->","0.80"}],",",RowBox[{"m","->","0.005"}],",",RowBox[{"Ne","->","500"}]}],
   "|>"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"Ag baseline\"","->",RowBox[{"N","[",RowBox[{"Ag1D","/.","params1D"}],"]"}]}],",",RowBox[{"\"r baseline\"","->",RowBox[{"N","[",RowBox[{"r1D","/.","params1D"}],"]"}]}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["Plot Ag over lag d and variance G. This is useful for the manuscript because it shows that local additivity declines near the optimum as d shrinks.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"agSurface1D","=",
  RowBox[{"Plot3D","[",
   RowBox[{RowBox[{"Evaluate","[",RowBox[{"Ag1D","/.","{Gsc->g}"}],"]"}],",",
    RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"d",",","0.01",",","3"}],"}"}],",",
    RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"g",",","0.05",",","3"}],"}"}],",",
    RowBox[{"AxesLabel","->","{\"d\",\"G\",\"Ag\"}"}],",",
    RowBox[{"PlotLabel","->","\"Additivity index in the one-trait moving-optimum model\""}],",",
    RowBox[{"Mesh","->","None"}],",",
    RowBox[{"ImageSize","->","Large"}]}],"]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["6. Two-trait gradient-rotation model", "Section"],
Cell["Here Ag can fall purely because the gradient rotates away from gmax, even when the variance magnitude and curvature magnitude are unchanged.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ClearAll","[",RowBox[{"lambda1",",","lambda2",",","gamma",",","theta",",","bnorm"}],"]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VlinRot","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"bnorm","^","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{RowBox[{"lambda1"," ",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Cos","[","theta","]"}],"^","2"}]}],"+",RowBox[{"lambda2"," ",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin","[","theta","]"}],"^","2"}]}]}],")"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VquadRot","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"gamma","^","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{RowBox[{"lambda1","^","2"}],"+",RowBox[{"lambda2","^","2"}]}],")"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"AgRot","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",RowBox[{"VlinRot","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"VlinRot","+","VquadRot"}],")"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"AgRot"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["Concrete parameter set", "Subsection"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"rotParams","=","{lambda1->2.0,lambda2->0.5,gamma->0.414,bnorm->1.0}"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"rotPlot","=",RowBox[{"Plot","[",
   RowBox[{RowBox[{"Evaluate","[",RowBox[{"AgRot","/.","rotParams"}],"]"}],",",
    RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"theta",",","0",",","Pi"}],"}"}],",",
    RowBox[{"AxesLabel","->","{\"theta\",\"Ag(theta)\"}"}],",",
    RowBox[{"PlotLabel","->","\"Gradient rotation can erode local additivity without changing total variance\""}],",",
    RowBox[{"ImageSize","->","Large"}]}],"]"}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["Solve numerically for the critical angle where Ag crosses 0.70.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"FindRoot","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"Evaluate","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"AgRot","/.","rotParams"}],"-","0.70"}],"]"}],"==","0"}],",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"theta",",","1.0"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]], "Input"],

Cell["7. Covariance-dynamics closure and the decomposition of r", "Section"],
Cell["This section gives you a symbolic place to manipulate the closure used in the draft. The goal is not to force Mathematica to simplify every expression fully, but to keep the structure explicit and editable.", "Text"],

Cell["Define the covariance route generically as Gdot = G H G - lambdaRec (G - GstarMat) + Mmat + Jmat - G/(2 Ne). Then derive the motion, environment, and covariance contributions to r at the level of symbolic matrix expressions.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ClearAll","[",RowBox[{"nq",",","bq",",","Gq",",","Hq",",","bdotu",",","Hdotu",",","uDot",",","GstarMat",",","Mmat",",","Jmat",",","lambdaR",",","Neff"}],"]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"nq","=","2"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"bq","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"b",",","nq"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Gq","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"gg",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"nq",",","nq"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Hq","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"hh",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"nq",",","nq"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"GstarMat","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"gs",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"nq",",","nq"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Mmat","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"mm",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"nq",",","nq"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Jmat","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"jj",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"nq",",","nq"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"bdotu","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"bu",",","nq"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Hdotu","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"hu",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"nq",",","nq"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VlinQ","=",RowBox[{"bq",".","Gq",".","bq"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VquadQ","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Hq",".","Gq"}],".",RowBox[{"Hq",".","Gq"}]}],"]"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"GdotQ","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Gq",".","Hq",".","Gq"}],"-",RowBox[{"lambdaR"," ",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"Gq","-","GstarMat"}],")"}]}],"+","Mmat","+","Jmat","-",RowBox[{"Gq","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"2"," ","Neff"}],")"}]}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"bdotQ","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Hq",".","Gq",".","bq"}],"+",RowBox[{"bdotu"," ","uDot"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VlinDotQ","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"2"," ",RowBox[{"bq",".","Gq",".","bdotQ"}]}],"+",RowBox[{"bq",".","GdotQ",".","bq"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VquadDotQ","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Gq",".","Hq",".","Gq"}],".","Hdotu"}],"]"}]," ","uDot","+",
     RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Hq",".","Gq",".","Hq"}],".","GdotQ"}],"]"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"rQ","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinDotQ","/","VlinQ"}],"-",RowBox[{"VquadDotQ","/","VquadQ"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"rQ"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["This cell keeps the full symbolic structure of the closure visible. In practice, for manuscript derivation work, you will often inspect parts of this expression rather than forcing Mathematica to compress the whole thing into one line.", "Text"],

Cell["Extract motion, environment, and covariance parts explicitly.", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"rMotion","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",
    RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"2"," ",RowBox[{"bq",".","Gq",".","Hq",".","Gq",".","bq"}]}],"/","VlinQ"}],"-",
      RowBox[{RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Hq",".","Gq",".","Hq"}],".",RowBox[{"Gq",".","Hq",".","Gq"}]}],"]"}],"/","VquadQ"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"rEnv","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",
    RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"2"," ",RowBox[{"bq",".","Gq",".","bdotu"}]," ","uDot"}],"/","VlinQ"}],"-",
      RowBox[{RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Gq",".","Hq",".","Gq"}],".","Hdotu"}],"]"}]," ","uDot","/","VquadQ"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"rCov","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",
    RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"bq",".","GdotQ",".","bq"}],"/","VlinQ"}],"-",
      RowBox[{RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Hq",".","Gq",".","Hq"}],".","GdotQ"}],"]"}],"/","VquadQ"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"rMotion\"","->","rMotion"}],",",RowBox[{"\"rEnv\"","->","rEnv"}],",",RowBox[{"\"rCov\"","->","rCov"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["The next natural step, if you want, is to substitute a low-dimensional concrete covariance system and explore sign patterns of rMotion, rEnv, and rCov across parameter ranges.", "Text"],

Cell["8. Introgression criterion", "Section"],
Cell["Now define a rank-1 incoming variance pulse Delta G = sigmaDelta^2 v v^T and compute the first-order shifts in Vlin and Vquad. This is the symbolic core of the introgression criterion.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ClearAll","[",RowBox[{"sigD",",","v"}],"]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"v","=",RowBox[{"Array","[",RowBox[{"vv",",","nq"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"deltaG","=",RowBox[{RowBox[{"sigD","^","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"Outer","[",RowBox[{"Times",",","v",",","v"}],"]"}]}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VlinPlus","=",RowBox[{"Expand","[",RowBox[{"bq",".",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"Gq","+","deltaG"}],")"}],".","bq"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"VquadPlus","=",RowBox[{"Expand","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"1","/","2"}]," ",RowBox[{"Tr","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"Hq",".",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"Gq","+","deltaG"}],")"}]}],".",RowBox[{"Hq",".",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"Gq","+","deltaG"}],")"}]}]}],"]"}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"dVlin1","=",RowBox[{"Normal","[",RowBox[{"Series","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinPlus","-","VlinQ"}],",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"sigD",",","0",",","2"}],"}"}]}],"]"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"dVquad1","=",RowBox[{"Normal","[",RowBox[{"Series","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VquadPlus","-","VquadQ"}],",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"sigD",",","0",",","2"}],"}"}]}],"]"}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"First-order shift in Vlin\"","->","dVlin1"}],",",RowBox[{"\"First-order shift in Vquad\"","->","dVquad1"}]}],"}"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["If the symbolic expansion is clean, Mathematica should show that the leading shift in Vlin is sigmaDelta^2 (b^T v)^2 and that the leading shift in Vquad is sigmaDelta^2 v^T H G H v.", "Text"],

Cell["A convenient first-order expression for the change in Ag is then", "Text"],
Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"deltaAg1","=",RowBox[{"FullSimplify","[",
    RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinQ","+","dVlin1"}],"/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{RowBox[{"VlinQ","+","dVlin1"}],"+",RowBox[{"VquadQ","+","dVquad1"}]}],")"}]}],"-",RowBox[{"VlinQ","/",RowBox[{"(",RowBox[{"VlinQ","+","VquadQ"}],")"}]}]}],"]"}]}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Series","[",RowBox[{"deltaAg1",",",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"sigD",",","0",",","2"}],"}"}]}],"]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["This is the right place to inspect the sign structure of introgression benefit versus curvature cost.", "Text"],

Cell["9. Export-quality plots and saving outputs", "Section"],
Cell["The next cells give you saveable examples. They export one 3D plot and one 2D rotation plot as PDFs to the same folder as the notebook when you edit the output path.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"outputDir","=","NotebookDirectory[]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Export","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"FileNameJoin","[",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"outputDir",",","\"Ag_surface_1trait.pdf\""}],"}"}],"]"}],",","agSurface1D"}],"]"}],";","\n",
  RowBox[{"Export","[",RowBox[{RowBox[{"FileNameJoin","[",RowBox[{"{",RowBox[{"outputDir",",","\"Ag_rotation_2trait.pdf\""}],"}"}],"]"}],",","rotPlot"}],"]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["What to record in your manuscript notes", "Section"],
Cell["For manuscript work, the most useful workflow is:\n\n1. Keep the symbolic definitions fixed.\n2. Change only the parameter blocks.\n3. Re-run the plotting cells.\n4. Copy the displayed formulas and numerical outputs into your notes.\n5. Use the Monte Carlo blocks whenever you want an independent check against the symbolic expressions.\n\nRecommended quantities to record explicitly:\n• Vlin, Vquad, and Ag for each worked example.\n• Corr[L, L+Q]^2 as a numerical check of the local R^2 claim.\n• The exact algebraic identity dAg/dt = Ag (1-Ag) r.\n• The one-trait formula Ag = d^2/(d^2 + G/2).\n• The gradient-rotation curve Ag(theta) and any critical exit angle theta*.\n• The symbolic structure of rMotion, rEnv, and rCov.\n• The first-order introgression shifts in Vlin, Vquad, and Ag.\n\nThat gives you a full symbolic-to-numerical audit trail.", "Text"],

Cell["Minimal troubleshooting", "Section"],
Cell["If a symbolic result does not simplify cleanly:\n• use FullSimplify[..., Assumptions -> {...}] with explicit positivity assumptions;\n• substitute numerical parameters early if the symbolic expression is too large;\n• use MatrixForm only for display, not for further algebra;\n• if Expectation becomes slow, replace it with Monte Carlo verification for that step.\n\nIf you want to extend the notebook further, the next natural additions are:\n• a moving-optimum time simulation with NDSolve;\n• an explicit two-trait environmental-rotation dynamical model;\n• a figure-matching export pipeline for manuscript plots;\n• a section that mirrors the SI appendix equation numbers directly.", "Text"]

},
